---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SignatureTool from '../components/SignatureTool.tsx';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Resize Signature Image for Online Forms | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Crop and resize a photo or scan of your signature to a size in KB in your browser. Capture tips, size guidance and why a tiny signature can miss a minimum.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: resize signature', '/resize-signature/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" title={title} description={description} path="/resize-signature/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <h1 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-bold">Resize a signature image</h1>
  <p class="mb-6">
    Add a photo or scan of your signature, crop it close, and reduce it to the file size your form asks for. The
    maximum starts at 20 KB, and you can change it or add a minimum.
  </p>

  <SignatureTool client:load defaultMaxKb={20} />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-3">
      The signature tool crops and compresses your image inside your browser, so it needs JavaScript. Turn it on and
      reload to continue. The guidance on this page about capturing a clean signature and choosing sizes works without
      it.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>Capture a clean signature first</h2>
    <p>
      The quality of the final file depends mostly on the starting picture. A clean source also compresses into fewer
      bytes, which matters when the limit is tight. A few habits help:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use plain white paper without lines, folds or printed text near the signature.</li>
      <li>Sign with a dark pen that makes a solid line, such as a black or dark blue gel or felt-tip pen. Pencil and pale ink fade out.</li>
      <li>Sign a little larger than usual and leave a margin of empty paper all around it.</li>
      <li>Photograph it in daylight, with the paper flat and your phone held directly above it so the page is not at an angle.</li>
      <li>Keep your own hand and phone from casting a shadow across the paper.</li>
      <li>Sign several times and use the best one rather than trying to repair a poor one afterwards.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Use only your own signature, and only on forms where you are the person signing.</p>

    <h2>Crop and position</h2>
    <p>
      Use the crop and positioning controls to cut away the empty paper so the signature fills most of the frame, with a
      small, even border. A tight crop means fewer pixels spent on blank paper and a larger signature at any given
      output size. Make sure no stroke touches the edge, since a cut-off flourish looks like a different signature.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the paper looks grey or yellow in the photo, retaking it in better light usually works better than heavy
      contrast editing. Strong contrast filters can turn thin strokes into jagged steps, and they add detail that has
      to be stored, which pushes the file size up.
    </p>

    <h2>Size and dimension guidance</h2>
    <p>
      Forms describe signature size in different ways: as a file size in KB, as pixels such as a width and height, or in
      centimetres. We do not publish a standard because there is none, and portals change their rules. Follow the exact
      words on your form.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>File size in KB</strong> is the number the tool works to. Set the maximum, and the minimum if the form
        gives one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pixels</strong> are set with the width and height fields. Turn on the aspect lock unless the form needs a
        ratio that differs from your crop, in which case crop to that shape first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Centimetres</strong> depend on print resolution, which an upload check usually cannot see. When a form
        gives centimetres and also gives pixels or KB, work from the pixels or KB.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Signatures are normally wide and short, so expect a wide rectangle. If the form fixes the pixel size, choose the
      quality-first option, because it keeps the dimensions you typed and lowers only the compression.
    </p>

    <h2>Why a tiny signature may not reach a minimum</h2>
    <p>
      Compression can make a file smaller, but it cannot invent detail to make a file larger. A signature that is only
      a few hundred pixels wide, drawn in one colour on a white background, holds very little information. A
      very small, simple image can stay under a few KB even at maximum quality, so a large minimum may be unreachable
      at that size.
    </p>
    <p>
      A larger, sharper source holds more information and has more room to reach a minimum. If you run into this limit, the choices are:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Start again from a larger photo or scan of the signature.</li>
      <li>Allow more pixels, if the form does not fix the dimensions. Enlarging adds bytes but no sharper detail.</li>
      <li>Check whether the form actually enforces the minimum, or whether only the maximum matters.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      When none of this works, the tool reports that the minimum was not reached. It will not pretend otherwise, and
      it does not add hidden filler to inflate the file.
    </p>

    <h2>Which file format to choose</h2>
    <p>
      JPEG is the common answer. A dark signature on white paper compresses well as a JPEG, and almost every upload form
      accepts it. PNG keeps edges perfectly crisp and can be small for a flat white background, but it may be larger
      than the JPEG for a photograph of paper with slight shading. WebP is offered where your browser can create it, and
      is only useful if your form lists it. A JPEG cannot hold transparency, so a signature with a see-through background
      is saved with a solid colour behind it.
    </p>

    <h2>Last look before you upload</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Zoom in on the preview and compare the thin ends of strokes with your original.</li>
      <li>Confirm the size and the pixel dimensions displayed, then compare them with your form.</li>
      <li>Keep the signature file apart from your photo file, since the two usually have different limits.</li>
      <li>Rename the downloaded file clearly, for example with the word signature in it, so you do not attach the wrong one.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The same process works for photographs. The <a href="/resize-image-to-20kb/">20 KB page</a> covers
      photos in detail, and the <a href="/guides/photo-size-for-online-forms/">guide to photo sizes</a> explains the
      terms used here. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> describes what happens to your signature image.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about signature images</h2>
    <h3>Do I need a scanner?</h3>
    <p>No. A phone photo in good light is enough, as long as the paper is flat and evenly lit.</p>
    <h3>Can I draw my signature on screen?</h3>
    <p>
      SizeTune works from an image file of a signature. It does not provide a drawing pad, so sign on paper and
      photograph it.
    </p>
    <h3>The result looks faint or grey. What went wrong?</h3>
    <p>
      Most often the ink was light or the paper was in shadow. Retake the photo with a darker pen and brighter, even
      light, then crop again.
    </p>
    <h3>Is my signature uploaded to SizeTune?</h3>
    <p>
      SizeTune's code does not send your image anywhere. The cropping and compression run in your browser. You decide
      where the downloaded file goes next.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I be sure the portal will accept the result?</h3>
    <p>
      No. We cannot see your portal's checks, so compare the displayed size, dimensions and format with what the form
      says before you submit. The <a href="/">home page tool</a> offers the same engine without the signature
      cropping.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
